    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. The codebase already had a `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper (used by the validation/forbidden/data-integrity/general handlers) that produces exactly this shape and, when returned as `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`, serializes as `application/problem+json`.\n\nThe rejection handlers that still returned ad-hoc bodies (plain `String` for the 409/429 conflicts, and `Map.of(\"errors\", \u2026)` for the 400s) were the only gap. I converted each to the established pattern:\n\n```java\nProblemDetail detail = ProblemDetails.build(ex, <STATUS>, ex.getMessage());\nresponse.send(ResponseEntity.status(<STATUS>).body(detail));\n```\n\n**Handlers changed** (each a self-contained single-method class, so WMC_other \u2248 0 and CC = 1 \u2014 the cheapest possible unit to edit):\n- 409: `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateEmail`, `DuplicateHousehold`, `DuplicateIdentity`, `DuplicateTelephone`\n- 429: `DailyLimitExceeded`\n- 400: `MissingOwnerFields`, `FutureRegistrationDate`, `InvalidOwnerEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`\n\n**Kept the structural impact low** by reusing the existing `ProblemDetails` helper rather than adding new abstractions \u2014 no class grew, no method gained complexity (each `handle` stays CC 1), and no wiring/YAML changed. `MethodArgumentNotValid`, `DataIntegrityViolation`, `NotFound` (404), `IdempotentReplay` (200 replay), and `Forbidden` (403) were already correct or out of scope and left untouched.\n\nVerified: the previously-failing `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (removes `city` \u2192 400) now returns `application/problem+json` with `$.status = 400`, and the full suite is green.",
